    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: Is This a Good Offer?\n\n**No, this is an underwhelming initial offer for a Senior PM (L6) in the Bay Area, especially for an IC6 moving from Meta.** \n\nAs presented, it represents a **lateral move or net pay cut**:\n\n* **Base Salary Cut:** Your guaranteed cash drops from **$240,000 to $215,000** (a $25,000 reduction).\n* **Annualized Total Compensation (TC):**\n  * **On a 4-year average:** Base ($215k) + Bonus ($43k) + RSUs ($200k/yr) = **$458,000/yr** (well below your current ~$526,000/yr).\n  * **Accounting for Google\u2019s Front-Loaded Vesting Schedule (typically 33% / 33% / 22% / 12%):**\n    * **Year 1:** $215k base + $43k bonus + $264k stock (33%) = **~$522,000** (essentially flat with Meta).\n    * **Year 2:** $215k base + $43k bonus + $264k stock (33%) = **~$522,000**.\n    * **Year 3:** $215k base + $43k bonus + $176k stock (22%) = **~$434,000** (steep drop).\n    * **Year 4:** $215k base + $43k bonus + $96k stock (12%) = **~$354,000** (severe drop).\n* **No Sign-on Bonus:** There is nothing to compensate for friction, forfeited unvested stock, or the base pay drop.\n* **The \"Equity Refresh\" Reality:** While Google refreshes stack, they depend on performance and market conditions. You should never accept a below-market initial package based solely on uncommitted future refreshes.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n* **Do not accept** the offer as-is.\n* **Negotiate firmly.** You have near-optimal leverage: you were an inbound reach-out, you are happy and thriving at Meta, and your current compensation exceeds their opening bid.\n* **Walk away** if Google refuses to meaningfully improve the package to beat your current run rate.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Ask For (and Target Numbers)\n\nTo make leaving your Meta IC6 role financially worthwhile, your Year 1 target should be **$600,000+**. Google has standard bandwidth to move within L6 bands for candidates with competing Big Tech packages.\n\n| Component | Google Initial Offer | Your Counter-Offer Target | Target Realistic Landing Zone |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $215,000 | **$250,000** | **$240,000 \u2013 $245,000** (Match/beat Meta) |\n| **Annual Bonus** | 20% ($43,000) | 20% (tied to base) | ~$48,000 \u2013 $49,000 |\n| **4-Yr RSUs (GSUs)** | $800,000 | **$1,050,000** | **$950,000 \u2013 $1,000,000** |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000** | **$50,000 \u2013 $60,000** |\n| **Year 1 Target TC** | ~$522,000 | **~$672,000** | **~$600,000 \u2013 $625,000** |\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Structure the Conversation\n\nWhen speaking with the recruiter, remain positive about the team and the problem space, but be clear and direct about the compensation gap.\n\n#### Key Talking Points:\n1. **Highlight the Base Disparity:** Frame taking a base pay cut from $240k to $215k as a non-starter.\n2. **Address the Total Value & Vesting Curve:** Point out that on an annualized basis, $800k over 4 years falls below your current Meta equity trajectory ($200k\u2013$250k/yr).\n3. **Ask for Sign-on to Bridge the Gap:** A sign-on bonus is the easiest lever for recruiters to pull without breaking compensation committee equity/base caps.\n\n#### Example Negotiation Script:\n\n> *\"Thank you for extending the offer. I really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m excited about the scope of the L6 role. However, looking closely at the compensation, this package would actually represent a step backward from my current run-rate at Meta.*\n>\n> *At Meta, my base is $240,000, my bonus target is roughly $36,000, and I have a steady vesting schedule delivering over $200,000\u2013$250,000 annually, putting my total comp around $526,000.*\n>\n> *Because I wasn't actively looking and am in a strong position where I am, I can't justify a move that reduces my base salary or leaves my total compensation flat. To make this move compelling, I\u2019d be looking for:*\n> * *A base salary of **$250,000** (aligning with the posted band and matching my current trajectory)*\n> * *An equity grant closer to **$1,050,000** over 4 years*\n> * *A sign-on bonus of **$75,000***\n>\n> *If we can get the package into that range, I would be thrilled to sign and give my notice at Meta.\"*",
